<section>
  <h2>How the 51 members are made (storylines)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Start:</b> today’s GFS analysis winds at 850, 700, 500 and 200 hPa (observed state, no forecast steps), with the seedling’s own swirl taken out. Bay sea temperature from NOAA OISST and the latest real-time MJO. No forecast model and no past-storm winds are used for the future.</li>
    <li><b>Storylines, not small nudges.</b> The environment will not stay as it is for a week. Thinking from today’s state (ridge near 19°N, strong north-east monsoon easterlies south of it, active MJO over the Indian Ocean, a warm Bay, strong El Niño and positive IOD), seven ways it could realistically change are written out as storylines: the ridge holds; weakens slowly; breaks under a westerly trough; a deep trough pulls a fast-strengthening storm; breaks then rebuilds; a strong storm pushes the ridge itself; or the system stays weak and sheared. Each storyline changes the winds day by day (ridge strength and position, south-westerlies in a ridge break, the trough moving east, monsoon surges, the MJO), and the storm can push the ridge back once it is strong.</li>
    <li><b>How many members each storyline gets</b> comes from today’s state and from what storms forming in the same place and season did in similar climate states (1980–2025: of 55 storms forming in the south/east Bay between mid-October and mid-November, 25% never hit land, 24% went to Bangladesh/Myanmar, 16% to Tamil Nadu and 24% to Andhra Pradesh; in El Niño years most went to Andhra Pradesh, Tamil Nadu or Bangladesh). The member counts are in the storyline table.</li>
    <li><b>Inside each storyline</b> the timing (up to ¾ day earlier or later), how strongly it happens (75–125%), formation time, start position, sea temperature, shear and storm size are spread evenly with a fixed sequence, so no two members are alike and nothing is random. Member 0 keeps today’s environment unchanged.</li>
    <li><b>Track and strength:</b> our steering model (fitted on every Bay storm since 1980) and our 12-hour strength model with inland decay.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
<section>
  <h2>How far to trust it</h2>
  <ul class="note">
    <li>The storylines and their member counts are our judgement from today’s state and past storms, not a forecast model’s output. Shares show how the outcomes split across these storylines; they are not calibrated probabilities.</li>
    <li>The track model on its own is good (in past storms’ real winds it placed landfall within about 1° of latitude, right coast stretch about half the time); the uncertainty is in which storyline happens.</li>
    <li>Strength: treat as one IMD category either way.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
</div>
